<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ClickHouse memory ceiling for the Langfuse data plane.

  Why this file exists. On 2026-10-02 the kernel OOM killer terminated
  clickhouse-server on a 3.9 GB VM shared with Redis, MinIO, the worker and the
  Cloud SQL proxy. Docker did not mark the container exited, so it stayed listed
  as "Up" with a failing healthcheck for five days; because langfuse-web runs
  ClickHouse migrations at startup and exits on failure, the outage reached the
  front door as a 503. Nothing alerted.

  What this changes. The server now refuses work under pressure instead of
  growing until the kernel kills it. The merge scheduler is the main consumer to
  be throttled, which is the intended trade: delayed merges degrade performance,
  whereas an OOM kill is an outage.

  Why the ceiling moved, 2026-10-15. The original 1.6 GB was correct in intent
  and unreachable in practice. The VM had 3.9 GB; the other four containers and
  the page cache already accounted for about 1.9 GB of it, so the 2 GB cgroup
  cap — this limit's safety net, set in docker-compose.yml — sat at what the host
  could actually hand over. ClickHouse was therefore killed while still inside
  its own limit: 76 OOM kills in one day, roughly ten minutes apart, each one
  recorded against the container's memory cgroup rather than the host's
  (`constraint=CONSTRAINT_MEMCG`), which is what identified the cap as the
  operative ceiling. A limit that the host cannot fund is not a limit; it is a
  countdown.

  What changed. The VM is e2-standard-2 (2 vCPU, 8 GB) and this ceiling scales
  with it, as the note below requires. 3 GB against a 4 GB cgroup leaves about
  1 GB for memory ClickHouse does not track itself — thread stacks, allocator
  arenas, and the page cache over the data directory. That margin is the actual
  fix: at 1.6 GB inside 2 GB there was roughly 400 MB, and it was consumed every
  ten minutes.

  Change this with the host size, not independently of it. Raising either this
  limit or the cgroup cap alone changes nothing, because the lower of the two
  governs.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!-- 3 GB -> 4 GB on 2026-10-17, moved with the cgroup cap in
         docker-compose.yml (4g -> 5g). The two must move together: the lower of
         them governs, and a ceiling the cgroup cannot fund is a countdown rather
         than a limit. The ~1 GB margin for what ClickHouse does not track itself
         — thread stacks, allocator arenas, page cache over the data directory —
         is deliberately unchanged, because it is that margin that was consumed
         at 1.6 GB inside 2 GB and again at 3 GB inside 4 GB.

         4 GB -> 5 GB later the same day, with the cgroup at 6g. At 4 GB the
         server's own tracked usage settled ABOVE its ceiling — 4.03 GiB of
         allocations against a 3.73 GiB maximum — and the overcommit tracker then
         refused everything: the Langfuse trace list and score reads died with
         Code 241 and the API reported "Request timed out", including queries over
         `system.parts` that return a handful of rows. The working set is elevated
         by a merge backlog (23 concurrent merges, ~970 parts) that the earlier
         restart loop created, so the ceiling has to sit above the merges' real
         footprint rather than exactly at the data's. The margin above the cgroup
         is raised with it, to ~1.34 GB. -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>5000000000</max_server_memory_usage>

    <!-- Keep the caches small relative to the ceiling above: together they
         would otherwise be allowed to occupy most of it. At 384 MB they are
         about 13% of the new ceiling, down from 24% of the old one. -->
    <mark_cache_size>268435456</mark_cache_size>
    <uncompressed_cache_size>134217728</uncompressed_cache_size>
</clickhouse>
